[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 746,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 576,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113942,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 576,
        "headingText": "<p>Oral melanoacanthoma is a non-neoplastic process that is distinct from the neoplastic cutaneous melanoacanthoma. It is characterized by a dual proliferation of epithelial and melanocytic cells with a characteristic clinical profile of rapid initial growth but indolent behaviour.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-12-05T15:19:10"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 747,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 577,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113943,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113944,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E90.3 Benign neoplasm of other or unspecified parts of mouth</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113945,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> melanoacanthosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113946,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113947,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The buccal mucosa is affected in approximately half of all cases, although it may occur on any oral mucosal surface, including the palate, gingiva&nbsp;/ alveolar mucosa, labial mucosa, or tongue {19254138; 27398186}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113948,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients with melanoacanthoma present with a rapidly growing, pigmented lesion that is usually smooth but can be slightly raised in appearance {19254138; 12544093}. It is most frequently asymptomatic, but mild symptoms such as pain, itching, or burning have been described {12544093}. Unifocal lesions are most common, with multifocal lesions occurring in a minority of cases {19254138; 27398186}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113949,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Melanoacanthoma occurs most frequently in people with higher natural levels of skin pigmentation. There is a female predominance. It affects patients of all ages, from children to the elderly, with an average age at presentation in the third to fifth decades of life {12544093; 19254138; 27398186}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113950,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although the etiology is unknown, multiple reactive factors have been proposed, including chronic trauma and reactions to oral care products such as toothpaste or mouthwash {27398186}. Oral melanoacanthoma has been rarely reported in association with Laugier&ndash;Hunziker syndrome and Addison disease {29450847; 28658350}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113951,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Although the pathogenesis is not proven, it is thought that chronic or acute trauma may stimulate melanocyte proliferation in oral melanoacanthoma in as many as 75% of cases {27398186}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113952,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pigmented mucosal tissue</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113953,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelial and melanocytic proliferation with acanthosis and dendritic melanocytes are present throughout the epithelium. Spongiosis and submucosal chronic inflammatory infiltrate are noted, and exocytosis of lymphocytes may also be seen {12544093}. Melanin deposition in the basal layer is frequent. Langerhans cells may also be prominent. Nuclear pleomorphism, hyperchromatism, and nesting of melanocytes are not present {19254138}. Immunohistochemistry for S100, HMB45, or melan-A highlights dendritic melanocytes {19254138; 23356913}. Mucosal melanoma is the most important differential diagnosis {27398186}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113954,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 588,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113955,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 588,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 761,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 591,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113956,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 591,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> dendritic melanocyte proliferation high in the epithelium; spongiosis; absence of atypia and invasion of melanocytic nests.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> epithelial acanthosis; melanin deposition in the basal layer.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 759,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 589,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113957,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 589,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 760,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 590,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 113958,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 328,
        "headingId": 590,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Oral melanoacanthoma frequently regresses after biopsy, even with incomplete removal {32856618; 12544093; 23356913}. Malignant transformation has not been described.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-26T14:52:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]